yoyo commit --ammend now refuses instead of committing. It's committed as 881fe81d, titled "Part of #1111".

What changed (src/commands_git.rs, in commit_args_from_tokens, which both the shell yoyo commit and REPL /commit go through):
• Unknown flags are refused. Any dash-led word commit doesn't know is refused before any git call. On the shell it exits 2. On the REPL nothing is committed, and that includes the /commit --ai path. Both doors print the same message: the usage line, the flag's name, a did-you-mean when one is close (--ammend → --amend), and "nothing was committed".
• `-m` / `--message` work like git's. -m fix x commits as fix x. A bare -m is refused with "needs a message".
• `--` ends flag parsing. -- --weird msg commits as --weird msg.
• Ordinary dashes stay message text: plain words, ->, -5, a lone -, and a quoted shell argument with a space in it like "-x thing". Only -x / --xy with a letter after the dashes and no whitespace counts as a flag, so rename foo -> bar isn't refused.

Tests:
• A new file, src/commands_git_commit_args_tests.rs, has 17 table rows, each checked on both doors, plus tests on the refusal message and the exit-2 path.
• A real-binary test in tests/unknown_flag_refusal.rs (it reuses that file's existing environment-scrubbing setup) uses a temp repo with a staged change. It shows commit --ammend exits 2 and leaves HEAD unchanged, and commit -m "fix x" commits with the subject fix x.
• As a positive control I disabled the flag check: exactly the two expected tests failed by name, and they passed again once restored. I made the change and the restore in one command, and the marker is gone.
• cargo test, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt --check all pass. src/commands_git.rs grew from 3858 to 3923 lines (+65), and I updated its entry in tests/module_size.rs with a note.

Docs: I added one line to docs/src/usage/commands.md.

Two small behaviour changes to know about:
• A -- inside a REPL message (/commit fix -- typo) is now taken as the separator and dropped, giving fix typo. Before, it was kept as text.
• --message=foo (with an =) isn't supported, so it's refused rather than committed.

